======================================================================================
2) REAL LIFE ANALOGY
======================================================================================

Imagine 5 judges deciding winner of a match.

Winner becomes official only if:
MAJORITY agrees.

So:
3 yes out of 5 = valid
2 yes out of 5 = invalid

Even if 2 judges are absent,
remaining 3 can still make decision.

This is exactly quorum majority in Raft.

Raft intuition:
“Leader is official only when majority accepts.”

======================================================================================
3) CORE IDEA OF RAFT
======================================================================================

Raft solves 3 big problems:

1) LEADER ELECTION
Choose one safe leader

2) LOG REPLICATION
Replicate writes in same order everywhere

3) SAFETY
Never lose committed data

This is the “3 points” Jordan likely wrote in:
“Why does it work?”

The famous 3 deep guarantees:
- Election Safety
- Log Matching
- Leader Completeness

We’ll go DEEP into these.

======================================================================================
4) NODE STATES IN RAFT
======================================================================================

Every node can be in 3 states:

1) FOLLOWER
Passive, waits for leader

2) CANDIDATE
Starts election when timeout happens

3) LEADER
Handles all writes + heartbeats

Why this design:
simple mental model

Tradeoff:
single leader bottleneck
but much easier correctness

When NOT to use:
- ultra write-heavy geo-distributed social feed counters
- conflict-tolerant systems
- eventually consistent workloads

======================================================================================
5) WHY LEADER ELECTION EXISTS
======================================================================================

WHY:
Without a single leader,
multiple nodes may accept writes.

That causes:
- divergent logs
- double booking
- split brain
- inconsistent state

So Raft first solves:
who is the valid writer?

This is why leader election is step zero.

======================================================================================
6) LEADER ELECTION FLOW (FULL DRY RUN)
======================================================================================

Cluster:
S1, S2, S3, S4, S5

All start as FOLLOWERS.

-----------------------------------
STEP 1: HEARTBEATS STOP
-----------------------------------

Leader crashes.
Followers stop receiving heartbeat.

Each follower has random election timeout:
150–300ms

Why random:
prevents simultaneous elections.

-----------------------------------
STEP 2: TIMEOUT EXPIRES
-----------------------------------

Suppose S3 timeout expires first.

S3 becomes CANDIDATE.

Actions:
- currentTerm++
- vote for self
- reset timeout
- send RequestVote RPC to all

Votes:
S3 = yes(self)

-----------------------------------
STEP 3: YES / NO LOGIC
-----------------------------------

Each follower checks:

A) Is candidate term >= my term?
B) Have I already voted this term?
C) Is candidate log at least as up-to-date?

If YES to all:
vote YES

Else:
vote NO

Suppose:
S1 yes
S2 yes
S4 no (already heard old delayed msg)
S5 yes

Total:
4/5 yes

S3 becomes LEADER.

This “yes/no” logic is SUPER IMPORTANT in interviews.

======================================================================================
7) WHY THE YES / NO RULES EXIST
======================================================================================

Rule A: TERM CHECK
Prevents old stale candidates from winning.

Rule B: ONE VOTE PER TERM
Ensures election safety.

Rule C: LOG UP-TO-DATE CHECK
Prevents incomplete node from becoming leader.

This is the heart of:
“Why does it work?”

Without rule C:
stale node may overwrite committed history.

Golden line:
“Raft elects the most up-to-date viable node.”

======================================================================================
8) HEARTBEATS AFTER LEADER ELECTION
======================================================================================

Once leader elected,
it sends AppendEntries heartbeat.

Purpose:
- prove liveness
- stop new elections
- replicate logs
- detect follower lag

Why this choice:
same RPC for heartbeat + replication
simpler protocol

Tradeoff:
leader network hotspot

======================================================================================
9) LOG REPLICATION (FULL FLOW)
======================================================================================

WHY:
Election alone is useless unless writes replicate safely.

Flow:
Client → Leader → Append log → replicate → majority ack → commit

Example:
SET x = 100

Leader log:
[term4, idx10, SET x=100]

Sent to followers.

Once majority stores:
entry COMMITTED.

Then applied to state machine.

Then leader replies success.

Critical:
reply only after majority.

That gives durability + linearizability.

======================================================================================
10) WHY RAFT WORKS — THE 3 CORE SAFETY PROOFS
======================================================================================

This is likely Jordan’s 3 points.

-----------------------------------
POINT 1: ELECTION SAFETY
-----------------------------------

At most ONE leader per term.

Why:
one node = one vote per term
majority quorums overlap

Proof intuition:
two leaders would need overlapping majority.
shared node cannot vote twice.

Hence impossible.

-----------------------------------
POINT 2: LOG MATCHING PROPERTY
-----------------------------------

If two logs contain same:
(index, term)

Then all previous entries identical.

Why:
AppendEntries includes prevLogIndex + prevLogTerm checks

So follower only appends if history matches.

This prevents divergence.

-----------------------------------
POINT 3: LEADER COMPLETENESS
-----------------------------------

Committed entry from old term
must appear in future leaders.

Why:
candidate needs most up-to-date log
majority overlap guarantees committed history survives

This is the BIGGEST reason Raft is trusted.

Golden line:
“Any committed entry is guaranteed to survive leadership changes.”

======================================================================================
11) FAILURE CASES
======================================================================================

-----------------------------------
A) LEADER CRASH
-----------------------------------

New election after timeout.
Majority elects new leader.

Recovery:
old leader rejoins as follower.

-----------------------------------
B) NETWORK PARTITION
-----------------------------------

5 nodes split:
3 | 2

3-side can elect leader.
2-side cannot.

This avoids split brain.

CAP:
Raft is CP.

Partition → minority unavailable.

-----------------------------------
C) STALE LEADER
-----------------------------------

Old leader isolated.
Still thinks leader.

When partition heals:
higher term heartbeat arrives
it steps down immediately.

-----------------------------------
D) DUPLICATE RETRIES
-----------------------------------

Client retries write after timeout.
Need idempotency key at app layer.

Raft ensures ordered replication,
not business dedupe.

-----------------------------------
E) REGION FAILURE
-----------------------------------

Cross-region Raft quorum survives
if majority regions alive.

Tradeoff:
higher commit latency.

======================================================================================
12) SPLIT BRAIN — HOW RAFT PREVENTS IT
======================================================================================

WHY THIS MATTERS:
Interviewers LOVE this.

Majority quorum overlap.

Example:
5 nodes

Majority = 3

Two different leaders need:
3 + 3 votes

Impossible without one common node.
That node cannot vote twice.

Hence split brain prevented.

This is THE quorum magic.

======================================================================================
13) SCALING BOTTLENECKS
======================================================================================

1) LEADER WRITE HOTSPOT
all writes through one node

2) CROSS REGION RTT
majority ack across regions expensive

3) LARGE LOG REPLAY
slow recovery for new nodes

4) SNAPSHOT COST
large state machine snapshot overhead

5) HOT PARTITIONS
one Raft group overloaded

Real-world fix:
shard into many Raft groups.

Example:
CockroachDB range-per-Raft-group
TiKV region-per-Raft-group

======================================================================================
14) TRADEOFFS
======================================================================================

Why choose Raft:
- understandable
- strong consistency
- easy leader semantics
- production proven

Problems solved:
- split brain
- stale writes
- failover correctness
- linearizable writes

Tradeoffs:
- higher write latency
- majority dependency
- single leader throughput limit
- partition hurts availability

When NOT to use:
- social feed likes
- analytics events
- Netflix watch counters
- eventually consistent systems

